            <ArticleTitle>Bowel endometriosis and schistosomiasis: a rare but possible association.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1060.e1-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the case of a patient submitted to laparoscopic bowel resection in whom histology revealed endometriosis and schistosomiasis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Multidisciplinary group practice and teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 31-year-old patient with pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparoscopic bowel resection.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparoscopic treatment of endometriosis affecting the sigmoid.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Histology performed on tissue removed at surgery revealed epithelioid granulomas with birefringent and partially calcified eggs characteristic of Schistosoma mansoni within an endometriotic lesion affecting the entire width of the sigmoid.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Endometriosis and schistosomiasis may be present simultaneously in patients with bowel symptoms and pelvic pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
